  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes a biomarker framework for neurodegeneration centered on a diseased neuron and its wider body-level readouts. In the middle, a neuron contains damaged mitochondria, released mitochondrial DNA, and pathogenic protein aggregates that also spread along the axon. Surrounding microglia indicate a linked inflammatory response. The diagram groups measurable signals into major categories: mitochondrial dysfunction, impaired mitophagy, inflammation, mtDNA release, altered glucose/oxygen metabolism, and abnormal protein aggregation. Each category is paired with example detection methods such as PET ligands, mass spectrometry, ELISA, CSF assays, PCR, and sequencing, suggesting how these pathological processes can be monitored in patients.\n\nThe right side expands this from the brain to systemic monitoring. A human silhouette highlights accessible tissues and pathways including blood, skeletal muscle, skin, vagus nerve, sensory neurons, and the gastrointestinal tract. This implies that disease-related mitochondrial and inflammatory changes may be tracked not only in the central nervous system but also in peripheral tissues. Overall, the figure presents neurodegeneration as a multisystem process in which mitochondrial damage, protein aggregation, and immune activation generate biomarker signals across brain, cerebrospinal fluid, imaging, and peripheral samples.",
